## ud6.3
ud6.3:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud6.3:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud6.3:1.3 | S: Now at that time the Buddha was sitting reviewing his own giving up of many bad, unskillful qualities, and the many skillful qualities he had fully developed. | T: And on that occasion the Blessed One sat reflecting on the various evil, unskillful qualities that had been abandoned [in him] and on the various skillful qualities that had gone to the culmination of their development.
ud6.3:2.1 | S: Then, knowing the many bad, unskillful qualities that he had given up and the many skillful qualities he had fully developed, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then as he realized the various evil, unskillful qualities that had been abandoned [in him] and the various skillful qualities that had gone to the culmination of their development, he on that occasion exclaimed:
ud6.3:3.1 | S: “What was before then was not; | T: Before, it was, then it wasn’t.
ud6.3:3.2 | S: what before was not then was. | T: Before, it wasn’t, then it was.
ud6.3:3.3 | S: It never was, nor will it be, | T: It wasn’t, won’t be,
ud6.3:3.4 | S: nor is it found today.” | T: & now isn’t to be found.
ud6.3:3.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud6.8
ud6.8:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud6.8:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha at the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
ud6.8:1.3 | S: Now at that time two gangs were both hopelessly in love with a certain courtesan. | T: And on that occasion two factions in Rājagaha were infatuated with a certain courtesan, their minds enthralled.
ud6.8:1.4 | S: Fighting, quarreling, and disputing, they attacked each other with fists, stones, rods, and swords, | T: Arguing, quarreling, & disputing, they attacked one another with fists, attacked one another with clods of dirt, attacked one another with sticks, attacked one another with knives,
ud6.8:1.5 | S: resulting in death and deadly pain. | T: so that they fell into death or death-like pain.
ud6.8:2.1 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Rājagaha for alms. | T: Then in the early morning, a large number of monks adjusted their under robes and–carrying their bowls & robes–went into Rājagaha for alms.
ud6.8:2.2 | S: Then, after the meal, when they returned from almsround, they went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what was happening. | T: Having gone for alms in Rājagaha, after the meal, returning from their alms round, they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side.
ud6.8:2.3 | P: Ekamantaṁ nisinnā kho te bhikkhū bhagavantaṁ etadavocuṁ: | T: As they were sitting there, they said to the Blessed One,
ud6.8:3.1 | P: “Idha, bhante, rājagahe dve pūgā aññatarissā gaṇikāya sārattā paṭibaddhacittā; | T: “At present, two factions in Rājagaha are infatuated with a certain courtesan, their minds enthralled.
ud6.8:3.2 | P: bhaṇḍanajātā kalahajātā vivādāpannā aññamaññaṁ pāṇīhipi upakkamanti, leḍḍūhipi upakkamanti, daṇḍehipi upakkamanti, satthehipi upakkamanti. | T: Arguing, quarreling, & disputing, they attack one another with fists, attack one another with clods of dirt, attack one another with sticks, attack one another with knives,
ud6.8:3.3 | P: Te tattha maraṇampi nigacchanti maraṇamattampi dukkhan”ti. | T: so that they fall into death or death-like pain.”
ud6.8:4.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud6.8:5.1 | S: “What has been attained and what is to be attained are both strewn over with dust for that one training while still sick. | T: What’s been attained, what’s to be attained, are both strewn with dust by one who trains in line with the afflicted.
ud6.8:5.2 | S: Those for whom the training is the essence, or precepts and observances, chastity, and service as the essence: this is one extreme. | T: Any precept & practice life whose essence is training, and the holy life whose essence is service: This is one extreme.
ud6.8:5.3 | S: Those who say, | T: Any who say,
ud6.8:5.4 | S: ‘There’s nothing wrong with sensual pleasures’: this is the second extreme. | T: “There’s no harm in sensual desires”: This, the second extreme.
ud6.8:5.5 | S: Thus these two extremes swell the charnel grounds, while the charnel grounds swell wrong view. | T: Both of these extremes cause the growth of cemeteries, and cemeteries cause views to grow.
ud6.8:5.6 | S: Not realizing these two extremes, some get stuck and some overreach. | T: Not directly knowing these two extremes, some fall short, some run too far.
ud6.8:5.7 | S: For those who realize these things, who were not found there, who did not conceive by that, there is no cycle of rebirths to be found.” | T: But those who, directly knowing them, didn’t exist there, didn’t construe by means of them: For them there’s no whirling through the cycle to be described.
ud6.8:5.8 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud6.9
ud6.9:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
