## ud7.1
ud7.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud7.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud7.1:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Sāriputta was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring Venerable Bhaddiya the Dwarf in many ways with a Dhamma talk. | T: At that time Ven. Sāriputta was–with a variety of approaches–instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf with Dhamma-talk.
ud7.1:2.1 | S: Then after being taught like this Bhaddiya’s mind was freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: As Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf was–with a variety of approaches–being instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged by Ven. Sāriputta with Dhamma-talk, his mind, through lack of clinging/sustenance, was released from the effluents.
ud7.1:3.1 | S: The Buddha saw what had happened. | T: The Blessed One saw that as Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf was–with a variety of approaches–being instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged by Ven. Sāriputta with Dhamma-talk, his mind, through lack of clinging/sustenance, was released from the effluents.
ud7.1:4.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud7.1:5.1 | S: “Above, below, everywhere free, | T: Above, below, everywhere released,
ud7.1:5.2 | S: not contemplating ‘I am this’. | T: he doesn’t focus on “I am this.”
ud7.1:5.3 | S: Freed like this, he has crossed the flood | T: Thus released, he crosses the flood
ud7.1:5.4 | S: not crossed before, so as to not be reborn.” | T: not crossed before, for the sake of no further becoming.
ud7.1:5.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud7.2
ud7.2:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud7.2:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud7.2:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Sāriputta was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring Venerable Bhaddiya the Dwarf in even more ways with a Dhamma talk, thinking that he was still a trainee. | T: At that time Ven. Sāriputta was–with a variety of approaches–instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf with Dhamma-talk to an even greater extent, as he thought that Bhaddiya was still just a learner.
ud7.2:2.1 | S: The Buddha saw what was happening. | T: The Blessed One saw that Ven. Sāriputta was–with a variety of approaches–instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf with Dhamma-talk to an even greater extent, as he thought that Bhaddiya was still just a learner.
ud7.2:3.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud7.2:4.1 | S: “They’ve cut the cycle, gone to the wishless; | T: He has cut the cycle, has gone away to freedom from longing.
ud7.2:4.2 | S: the streams are dried, they flow no more. | T: The dried-up stream no longer flows.
ud7.2:4.3 | S: Cut, the cycle no longer spins. | T: The cycle, cut, no longer turns.
ud7.2:4.4 | S: Just this is the end of suffering.” | T: This, just this, is the end of stress.
ud7.2:4.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud7.3
ud7.3:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud7.3:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud7.3:1.3 | S: Now at that time most of the people in Sāvatthī overly clung to sensual pleasures. Lustful, greedy, tied, infatuated, they lived completely addicted to sensual pleasures. | T: And on that occasion, most of the people in Sāvatthī were excessively attached to sensual pleasures. They lived infatuated with, greedy for, addicted to, fastened to, absorbed in sensual pleasures.
ud7.3:2.1 | S: Then several mendicants robed up in the morning and, taking their bowls and robes, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then in the early morning, a large number of monks adjusted their under robes and–carrying their bowls & robes–went into Sāvatthī for alms.
ud7.3:2.2 | S: Then, after the meal, when they returned from almsround, they went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what was happening. | T: Having gone for alms in Sāvatthī, after the meal, returning from their alms round, they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side.
ud7.3:2.3 | P: Ekamantaṁ nisinnā kho te bhikkhū bhagavantaṁ etadavocuṁ: | T: As they were sitting there, they said to the Blessed One,
ud7.3:2.4 | P: “idha, bhante, sāvatthiyā manussā yebhuyyena kāmesu ativelaṁ sattā rattā giddhā gathitā mucchitā ajjhosannā sammattakajātā kāmesu viharantī”ti. | T: “Most of the people in Sāvatthī are excessively attached to sensual pleasures. They live infatuated with, greedy for, addicted to, fastened to, absorbed in sensual pleasures.”
ud7.3:3.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud7.3:4.1 | S: “Clinging to sensual pleasures, <j>to the chains of the senses, | T: Attached to sensual pleasures, attached to sensual ties,
ud7.3:4.2 | S: blind to the faults of the fetters, | T: seeing no blame in the fetter,
ud7.3:4.3 | S: clinging to the chain of the fetters, | T: never will those attached to the fetter, the tie,
ud7.3:4.4 | S: they’ll never cross the flood so vast.” | T: cross over the flood so great & wide.
ud7.3:4.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud7.4
ud7.4:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud7.4:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud7.4:1.3 | S: Now at that time most of the people in Sāvatthī clung to sensual pleasures. Lustful, greedy, tied, infatuated, attached, and blinded, they lived completely addicted to sensual pleasures. | T: And on that occasion, most of the people in Sāvatthī were excessively attached to sensual pleasures. They lived infatuated with, greedy for, addicted to, fastened to, absorbed in sensual pleasures.
ud7.4:2.1 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then early in the morning the Blessed One adjusted his under robe and–carrying his bowl & robes–went into Sāvatthī for alms.
ud7.4:2.2 | S: He saw how attached the humans were. | T: He saw that most of the people in Sāvatthī were excessively attached to sensual pleasures, that they live infatuated with, greedy for, addicted to, fastened to, absorbed in sensual pleasures.
ud7.4:3.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud7.4:4.1 | S: “Blinded by sensual pleasures, wrapped in a net, | T: Blinded by sensuality covered by the net,
ud7.4:4.2 | S: they are smothered over by craving; | T: veiled with the veil of craving,
ud7.4:4.3 | S: bound by the kinsman of the negligent, | T: bound by the Kinsman of the heedless,
ud7.4:4.4 | S: like a fish caught in a funnel-net trap. | T: like fish in the mouth of a trap,
ud7.4:4.5 | S: They chase old age and death, | T: they go to aging & death,
ud7.4:4.6 | S: like a suckling calf its mother.” | T: like a milk-drinking calf to its mother.
ud7.4:4.7 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud7.5
ud7.5:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud7.5:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud7.5:1.3 | S: Then Venerable Bhaddiya the Dwarf, closely following several mendicants, approached the Buddha. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf, following behind a large number of monks, was going to the Blessed One.
ud7.5:2.1 | S: The Buddha saw Venerable Bhaddiya coming off in the distance—ugly, unsightly, deformed, and despised by most of the mendicants. | T: From afar, the Blessed One saw Ven. Bhaddiya the Dwarf coming, following behind a large number of monks: ugly, unsightly, stunted, treated with condescension by most of the monks.
ud7.5:2.2 | S: The Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: On seeing him, the Blessed One addressed the monks,
ud7.5:3.1 | S: “Mendicants, do you see this monk coming—ugly, unsightly, deformed, and despised by most of the mendicants?” | T: “Monks, do you see that monk coming from afar, following behind a large number of monks: ugly, unsightly, stunted, treated with condescension by most of the monks?”

