ud7.5:3.2 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
ud7.5:4.1 | S: “That mendicant is very mighty and powerful. It’s not easy to find an attainment that he has not already attained. | T: “That, monks, is a monk of great power, great might. The attainment already attained by that monk is not of a sort easily attained.
ud7.5:4.2 | S: And he has realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lives having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness.” | T: And by means of it he has reached & remains in the supreme goal of the holy life for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, knowing & realizing it for himself right in the here-&-now.”
ud7.5:5.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud7.5:6.1 | S: “With faultless parts and white canopy, | T: Faultless, canopied in white,
ud7.5:6.2 | S: the one-spoked chariot rolls on. | T: the single-spoked chariot rolls along.
ud7.5:6.3 | S: See it come, untroubled, | T: See him coming, untroubled:
ud7.5:6.4 | S: with stream cut, unbound.” | T: one whose stream is cut, free from bonds.
ud7.5:6.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud7.8
ud7.8:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud7.8:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud7.8:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Mahākaccāna was sitting not far from the Buddha, cross-legged, with his body straight and mindfulness of the body well-established internally on the present. | T: Now at that time Ven. Mahā Kaccāna was sitting not far from the Blessed One, his legs crossed, his body held erect, having mindfulness immersed in the body well-established to the fore within.
ud7.8:2.1 | S: The Buddha saw him meditating there. | T: The Blessed One saw Ven. Mahā Kaccāna sitting not far away, his legs crossed, his body held erect, having mindfulness immersed in the body well-established to the fore within.
ud7.8:3.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud7.8:4.1 | S: “Their mindfulness would always | T: If one were to have mindfulness always
ud7.8:4.2 | S: be established in the body, constant: | T: established, continually immersed in the body,
ud7.8:4.3 | S: ‘It might not be, and it might not be mine, | T: (thinking,) “It should not be, it should not be mine;
ud7.8:4.4 | S: It will not be, and it will not be mine.’ | T: it will not be, it will not be mine”–
ud7.8:4.5 | S: Meditating stage by stage on that, | T: there, in that step-by-step dwelling,
ud7.8:4.6 | S: in time they’d cross over clinging.” | T: one in no long time would cross over attachment. In AN 10:29, the Buddha recommends this view as conducive to developing dispassion for becoming. However, in MN 106 he warns that it can lead to the refined equanimity of the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, which can become an object of clinging. Only if that subtle clinging is detected can all clinging be abandoned. The Canon’s most extended discussion of this theme of meditation is in SN 22:55. See Appendix Two. For more on this topic, see The Paradox of Becoming, chapter 5.
ud7.8:4.7 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud7.9
ud7.9:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud7.9:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Mallas together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants when he arrived at a brahmin town of the Mallas named Thūṇa. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was wandering among the Mallans, together with a large community of monks, and came to a brahman village of the Mallans named Thūna.
ud7.9:1.3 | S: The brahmins and householders of Thūṇa heard: | T: The brahman householders of Thūna heard that
ud7.9:1.4 | S: “It seems the ascetic Gotama—a Sakyan, gone forth from a Sakyan family—while wandering in the land of the Mallas has arrived at Thūṇa, together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants.” | T: “Gotama the Sakyan contemplative, gone forth from the Sakyan clan, is wandering among the Mallans together with a large community of monks, and has arrived at Thūna.”
ud7.9:1.5 | S: They filled the well with grass and hay right to the top, thinking, | T: So they filled the well all the way to the brim with grass & chaff, [thinking],
ud7.9:1.6 | S: “Don’t let these shavelings, these fake ascetics drink the water.” | T: “Don’t let these shaven-headed contemplatives draw drinking water.”
ud7.9:2.1 | S: And then the Buddha left the road, went to the root of a tree, and sat down on the seat spread out. | T: Then the Blessed One, going down from the road, went to a certain tree, and on arrival sat down on a seat laid out.
ud7.9:2.2 | S: When he was seated he said to Venerable Ānanda: | T: Seated, he said to Ven. Ānanda:
ud7.9:2.3 | S: “Go on then, Ānanda, fetch me some water from that well.” | T: “Please, Ānanda, fetch me some drinking water from that well.”
ud7.9:3.1 | S: When he said this, Venerable Ānanda said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Ven. Ānanda replied,
ud7.9:3.2 | S: “Just now, sir, the brahmins and householders of Thūṇa filled the well with grass and hay right to the top, thinking, | T: “Just now, lord, the brahman householders of Thūna filled that well all the way to the brim with grass & chaff, [thinking],
ud7.9:3.3 | S: ‘Don’t let these shavelings, these fake ascetics drink the water.’” | T: ‘Don’t let these shaven-headed contemplatives draw drinking water.’”
ud7.9:4.1 | S: For a second time … | T: A second time, the Blessed One said to Ven. Ānanda: “Please, Ānanda, fetch me some drinking water from that well.” A second time, Ven. Ānanda replied, “Just now, lord, the brahman householders of Thūna filled that well all the way to the brim with grass & chaff, [thinking], ‘Don’t let these shaven-headed contemplatives draw drinking water.’”
ud7.9:4.2 | S: For a third time, the Buddha said to Ānanda: | T: A third time, the Blessed One said to Ven. Ānanda:
ud7.9:4.3 | S: “Go on then, Ānanda, fetch me some water from that well.” | T: “Please, Ānanda, fetch me some drinking water from that well.”
ud7.9:4.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied Ānanda. Taking his bowl he went to the well. | T: Responding, “As you say, lord,” to the Blessed One, Ven. Ānanda–taking a bowl–went to the well.
ud7.9:4.5 | S: As he approached the well, all the grass and hay erupted out of the well-mouth. The water stood transparent, unclouded, and clear right up to the top, seeming to overflow. | T: As he was approaching the well, it expelled all the grass & chaff from its mouth and stood filled to the brim–streaming, as it were–with pristine water, undisturbed & clear.
ud7.9:5.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda thought, | T: The thought occurred to him,
ud7.9:5.2 | S: “Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! The Realized One has such psychic power and might! | T: “How amazing! How astounding!–the great power & great might of the Tathāgata!–
ud7.9:5.3 | S: For when I approached this well, all the grass and hay erupted out of the well-mouth. The water stood transparent, unclouded, and clear right up to the top, seeming to overflow.” | T: in that, while I was approaching the well, it expelled all the grass & chaff from its mouth and stood filled to the brim–streaming, as it were–with pristine water, undisturbed & clear.”
ud7.9:5.4 | S: Gathering a bowl of drinking water he went back to the Buddha, and said to him, | T: Taking drinking water in his bowl, he went to the Blessed One and on arrival said,
ud7.9:5.5 | S: “It’s incredible, sir, it’s amazing! The Realized One has such psychic power and might! | T: “How amazing, lord! How astounding!–the great power & great might of the Tathāgata!–
ud7.9:5.6 | S: For when I approached that well, all the grass and hay erupted out of the well-mouth. The water stood transparent, unclouded, and clear right up to the top, seeming to overflow. | T: in that, while I was approaching the well, it expelled all the grass & chaff from its mouth and stood filled to the brim–streaming, as it were–with pristine water, undisturbed & clear.
ud7.9:5.7 | S: Drink the water, Blessed One! Drink the water, Holy One!” | T: Drink the water, O Blessed One! Drink the water, O One-Well-Gone!”
ud7.9:6.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud7.9:7.1 | S: “What difference would a well make | T: What need for a well
ud7.9:7.2 | S: if water is there all the time? | T: if there were waters always?
ud7.9:7.3 | S: Having cut off craving at the root, | T: Having cut craving by the root,
ud7.9:7.4 | S: who would go out on a quest?” | T: one would go about searching for what?
ud7.9:7.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud8.1
ud8.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud8.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud8.1:1.3 | S: Now at that time the Buddha was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the mendicants with a Dhamma talk about extinguishment. | T: And on that occasion the Blessed One was instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging the monks with Dhamma-talk concerned with unbinding.
ud8.1:1.4 | S: And those mendicants were paying attention, applying the mind, concentrating wholeheartedly, and actively listening. | T: The monks–receptive, attentive, focusing their entire awareness, lending ear–listened to the Dhamma.
ud8.1:2.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud8.1:3.1 | S: “There is, mendicants, that dimension where there is no earth, no water, no fire, no wind; no dimension of infinite space, no dimension of infinite consciousness, no dimension of nothingness, no dimension of neither perception nor non-perception; no this world, no other world, no moon or sun. | T: There is that dimension, monks, where there is neither earth, nor water, nor fire, nor wind; neither dimension of the infinitude of space, nor dimension of the infinitude of consciousness, nor dimension of nothingness, nor dimension of neither perception nor non-perception; neither this world, nor the next world, nor sun, nor moon.
ud8.1:3.2 | S: There, mendicants, I say there is no coming or going or remaining or passing away or reappearing. | T: And there, I say, there is neither coming, nor going, nor staying; neither passing away nor arising:
ud8.1:3.3 | S: It is not established, does not proceed, and has no support. | T: unestablished, unevolving, without support [mental object].
ud8.1:3.4 | S: Just this is the end of suffering.” | T: This, just this, is the end of stress.
ud8.1:3.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud8.2
ud8.2:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud8.2:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud8.2:1.3 | S: Now at that time the Buddha was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the mendicants with a Dhamma talk about extinguishment. | T: And on that occasion the Blessed One was instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging the monks with Dhamma-talk concerned with unbinding.
ud8.2:1.4 | S: And those mendicants were paying attention, applying the mind, concentrating wholeheartedly, and actively listening. | T: The monks–receptive, attentive, focusing their entire awareness, lending ear–listened to the Dhamma.
ud8.2:2.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud8.2:3.1 | S: “It’s hard to see what they call the ‘uninclined’, | T: It’s hard to see the unaffected,
ud8.2:3.2 | S: for the truth is not easy to see. | T: for the truth is not easily seen.
ud8.2:3.3 | S: For one who has penetrated craving, | T: Craving is pierced
ud8.2:3.4 | S: who knows and sees, there is nothing.” | T: in one who knows; for one who sees, there is nothing.
ud8.2:3.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud8.3
ud8.3:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud8.3:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud8.3:1.3 | S: Now at that time the Buddha was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the mendicants with a Dhamma talk about extinguishment. | T: And on that occasion the Blessed One was instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging the monks with Dhamma-talk concerned with unbinding.
ud8.3:1.4 | S: And those mendicants were paying attention, applying the mind, concentrating wholeheartedly, and actively listening. | T: The monks–receptive, attentive, focusing their entire awareness, lending ear–listened to the Dhamma.
ud8.3:2.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud8.3:3.1 | S: “There is, mendicants, freedom from rebirth, freedom from what has been produced, made, and conditioned. | T: There is, monks, an unborn–unbecome–unmade–unfabricated.
ud8.3:3.2 | S: If there were no freedom from rebirth, freedom from what has been produced, made, and conditioned, then you would find no escape here from rebirth, from what has been produced, made, and conditioned. | T: If there were not that unborn–unbecome–unmade–unfabricated, there would not be the case that escape from the born–become–made–fabricated would be discerned.
ud8.3:3.3 | S: But since there is freedom from rebirth, freedom from what has been produced, made, and conditioned, an escape is found from rebirth, from what has been produced, made, and conditioned.” | T: But precisely because there is an unborn–unbecome–unmade–unfabricated, escape from the born–become–made–fabricated is discerned.
ud8.3:3.4 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud8.4
ud8.4:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud8.4:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud8.4:1.3 | S: Now at that time the Buddha was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the mendicants with a Dhamma talk about extinguishment. | T: And on that occasion the Blessed One was instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging the monks with Dhamma-talk concerned with unbinding.
ud8.4:1.4 | S: And those mendicants were paying attention, applying the mind, concentrating wholeheartedly, and actively listening. | T: The monks–receptive, attentive, focusing their entire awareness, lending ear–listened to the Dhamma.
ud8.4:2.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud8.4:3.1 | S: “For the dependent there is quivering. For the independent there’s no quivering. | T: One who is dependent has wavering. One who is independent has no wavering.
ud8.4:3.2 | S: When there’s no quivering there is tranquility. | T: There being no wavering, there is calm.
ud8.4:3.3 | S: When there is tranquility there’s no inclination. | T: There being calm, there is no yearning.
ud8.4:3.4 | S: When there’s no inclination, there’s no coming and going. | T: There being no yearning, there is no coming or going.
ud8.4:3.5 | S: When there’s no coming and going, there’s no passing away and reappearing. | T: There being no coming or going, there is no passing away or arising.
ud8.4:3.6 | S: When there’s no passing away and reappearing there’s no this life or the next or between the two. | T: There being no passing away or arising, there is neither a here nor a there nor a between-the-two.
ud8.4:3.7 | S: Just this is the end of suffering.” | T: This, just this, is the end of stress.
ud8.4:3.8 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud8.6
ud8.6:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that on one occasion,
ud8.6:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Magadhans together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants when he arrived at the village of Pāṭali. | T: while the Blessed One was wandering among the Magadhans with a large community of monks, he arrived at Pāṭali Village.
ud8.6:1.3 | S: The lay followers of Pāṭali Village heard that he had arrived. | T: The lay followers of Pāṭali Village heard,
ud8.6:1.4 | P: “bhagavā kira magadhesu cārikaṁ caramāno mahatā bhikkhusaṅghena saddhiṁ pāṭaligāmaṁ anuppatto”ti. | T: “The Blessed One, they say, while wandering among the Magadhans with a large community of monks, has reached Pāṭali Village.”
ud8.6:1.5 | S: So they went to see him, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: So they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side.
ud8.6:1.6 | P: Ekamantaṁ nisinnā kho pāṭaligāmiyā upāsakā bhagavantaṁ etadavocuṁ: | T: As they were sitting there, they said to him,
ud8.6:1.7 | S: “Sir, please consent to come to our guest house.” | T: “Lord, may the Blessed One acquiesce to (the use of) the rest-house hall.”
ud8.6:1.8 | S: The Buddha consented with silence. | T: The Blessed One acquiesced with silence.
ud8.6:2.1 | S: Then, knowing that the Buddha had consented, the lay followers of Pāṭali Village rose from their seats, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on their right. Then they went to the guest house, where they spread carpets all over, prepared seats, set up a water jar, and placed an oil lamp. Then they went back to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and told him of their preparations, saying: | T: Sensing his acquiescence, the lay followers of Pāṭali Village got up from their seats, bowed down to him, circled him to the right, and then went to the rest-house hall. On arrival, they spread it all over with felt rugs, arranged seats, set out a water vessel, and raised an oil lamp. Then they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down, stood to one side. As they were standing there they said to him,
ud8.6:2.2 | P: “sabbasantharisanthataṁ, bhante, āvasathāgāraṁ; āsanāni paññattāni; udakamaṇiko patiṭṭhāpito telappadīpo āropito. | T: “Lord, the rest-house hall has been covered all over with felt rugs, seats have been arranged, a water vessel has been set out, and an oil lamp raised.
ud8.6:2.3 | S: “Please, sir, come at your convenience.” | T: May the Blessed One do what you think it is now time to do.”
ud8.6:3.1 | S: In the morning, the Buddha robed up and, taking his bowl and robe, went to the guest house together with the Saṅgha of mendicants. Having washed his feet, he entered the guest house and sat against the central column facing east. | T: So the Blessed One, adjusting his under robe and–carrying his bowl & robes–went together with a community of monks to the rest-house hall. On arrival he washed his feet, entered the hall, and sat with his back to the central post, facing east.
ud8.6:3.2 | S: The Saṅgha of mendicants also washed their feet, entered the guest house, and sat against the west wall facing east, with the Buddha right in front of them. | T: The community of monks washed their feet, entered the hall, and sat with their backs to the western wall, facing east, ranged around the Blessed One.
ud8.6:3.3 | S: The lay followers of Pāṭali Village also washed their feet, entered the guest house, and sat against the east wall facing west, with the Buddha right in front of them. | T: The lay followers of Pāṭali Village washed their feet, entered the hall, and sat with their backs to the eastern wall, facing west, ranged around the Blessed One.
ud8.6:3.4 | S: Then the Buddha addressed them: | T: Then the Blessed One addressed the lay followers of Pāṭali Village,
ud8.6:4.1 | S: “Householders, there are these five drawbacks for an unethical person because of their failure in ethics. | T: “Householders, there are these five drawbacks coming from an unvirtuous person’s failure in virtue.
ud8.6:4.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
ud8.6:4.3 | S: Firstly, an unethical person loses great wealth on account of negligence. | T: “There is the case where an unvirtuous person, by reason of heedlessness, undergoes the loss/confiscation of great wealth.
ud8.6:4.4 | S: This is the first drawback. | T: This is the first drawback coming from an unvirtuous person’s failure in virtue.
ud8.6:5.1 | S: Furthermore, an unethical person gets a bad reputation. | T: “Furthermore, the bad reputation of the unvirtuous person, failing in virtue, gets spread about.
ud8.6:5.2 | S: This is the second drawback. | T: This is the second drawback coming from an unvirtuous person’s failure in virtue.
ud8.6:6.1 | S: Furthermore, an unethical person enters any kind of assembly unconfident and embarrassed, whether it’s an assembly of aristocrats, brahmins, householders, or ascetics. | T: “Furthermore, whatever assembly the unvirtuous person, failing in virtue, approaches–whether of noble warriors, brahmans, householders, or contemplatives–he/she does so without confidence & abashed.
ud8.6:6.2 | S: This is the third drawback. | T: This is the third drawback coming from an unvirtuous person’s failure in virtue.
ud8.6:7.1 | S: Furthermore, an unethical person feels lost when they die. | T: “Furthermore, the unvirtuous person, failing in virtue, dies confused.
ud8.6:7.2 | S: This is the fourth drawback. | T: This is the fourth drawback coming from an unvirtuous person’s failure in virtue.
ud8.6:8.1 | S: Furthermore, an unethical person, when their body breaks up, after death, is reborn in a place of loss, a bad place, the underworld, hell. | T: “Furthermore, the unvirtuous person, failing in virtue–on the break-up of the body, after death–reappears in a plane of deprivation, a bad destination, a lower realm, a hell.
ud8.6:8.2 | S: This is the fifth drawback. | T: This is the fifth drawback coming from an unvirtuous person’s failure in virtue.
ud8.6:8.3 | S: These are the five drawbacks for an unethical person because of their failure in ethics. | T: “These, householders, are the five drawbacks coming from an unvirtuous person’s failure in virtue.
ud8.6:9.1 | S: There are these five benefits for an ethical person because of their accomplishment in ethics. | T: “Householders, there are these five rewards coming from a virtuous person’s consummation in virtue.
ud8.6:9.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
ud8.6:9.3 | S: Firstly, an ethical person gains great wealth on account of diligence. | T: “There is the case where a virtuous person, by reason of heedfulness, acquires a great mass of wealth.
ud8.6:9.4 | S: This is the first benefit. | T: This is the first reward coming from a virtuous person’s consummation in virtue.
ud8.6:10.1 | S: Furthermore, an ethical person gets a good reputation. | T: “Furthermore, the fine reputation of the virtuous person, consummate in virtue, gets spread about.
ud8.6:10.2 | S: This is the second benefit. | T: This is the second reward coming from a virtuous person’s consummation in virtue.
ud8.6:11.1 | S: Furthermore, an ethical person enters any kind of assembly bold and self-assured, whether it’s an assembly of aristocrats, brahmins, householders, or ascetics. | T: “Furthermore, whatever assembly the virtuous person, consummate in virtue, approaches–whether of noble warriors, brahmans, householders, or contemplatives–he/she does so with confidence & unabashed.
ud8.6:11.2 | S: This is the third benefit. | T: This is the third reward coming from a virtuous person’s consummation in virtue.
ud8.6:12.1 | S: Furthermore, an ethical person dies not feeling lost. | T: “Furthermore, the virtuous person, consummate in virtue, dies unconfused.
ud8.6:12.2 | S: This is the fourth benefit. | T: This is the fourth reward coming from a virtuous person’s consummation in virtue.
ud8.6:13.1 | S: Furthermore, when an ethical person’s body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm. | T: “Furthermore, the virtuous person, consummate in virtue–on the break-up of the body, after death–reappears in a good destination, a heavenly world.
ud8.6:13.2 | S: This is the fifth benefit. | T: This is the fifth reward coming from a virtuous person’s consummation in virtue.
ud8.6:13.3 | S: These are the five benefits for an ethical person because of their accomplishment in ethics.” | T: “These, householders, are the five rewards coming from a virtuous person’s consummation in virtue.”
ud8.6:14.1 | S: The Buddha spent much of the night educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the lay followers of Pāṭali Village with a Dhamma talk. Then he sent them off, | T: Then the Blessed One–having instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged the lay followers of Pāṭali Village for a large part of the night with Dhamma-talk–dismissed them, saying,
ud8.6:14.2 | S: “The night is getting late, householders. Please go at your convenience.” | T: “The night is far gone, householders. Do what you think it is now time to do.”
ud8.6:14.3 | S: And then the lay followers of Pāṭali Village approved and agreed with what the Buddha said. They got up from their seat, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on their right, before leaving. | T: So the lay followers of Pāṭali Village, delighting in & approving of the Blessed One’s words, got up from their seats, bowed down to him, and left, circling him to the right.
ud8.6:14.4 | S: Soon after they left the Buddha entered a private cubicle. | T: Then the Blessed One, not long after they had left, entered an empty building.
ud8.6:15.1 | S: Now at that time the Magadhan ministers Sunidha and Vassakāra were building a citadel at Pāṭali Village to keep the Vajjis out. | T: Now, on that occasion, Sunīdha & Vassakāra, the chief ministers of Magadha, were building a city at Pāṭali Village to preempt the Vajjians.
ud8.6:15.2 | S: At that time thousands of deities were taking possession of building sites in Pāṭali Village. | T: And on that occasion many devas by the thousands were occupying sites in Pāṭali Village.
ud8.6:15.3 | S: Illustrious rulers or royal chief ministers inclined to build houses at sites possessed by illustrious deities. | T: In the area where devas of great influence occupied sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of great influence were inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:15.4 | S: Middling rulers or royal chief ministers inclined to build houses at sites possessed by middling deities. | T: In the area where devas of middling influence occupied sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of middling influence were inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:15.5 | S: Lesser rulers or royal chief ministers inclined to build houses at sites possessed by lesser deities. | T: In the area where devas of low influence occupied sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of low influence were inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:16.1 | S: With clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman, the Buddha saw those deities taking possession of building sites in Pāṭali Village, | T: The Blessed One, with the divine eye–purified and surpassing the human–saw many devas by the thousands occupying sites in Pāṭali Village.
ud8.6:16.2 | S: and the people building houses in accord with the station of the deities. | T: In the area where devas of great influence occupied sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of great influence were inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:16.3 | P: Yasmiṁ padese majjhimā devatā vatthūni pariggaṇhanti, majjhimānaṁ tattha raññaṁ rājamahāmattānaṁ cittāni namanti nivesanāni māpetuṁ. | T: In the area where devas of middling influence occupied sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of middling influence were inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:16.4 | P: Yasmiṁ padese nīcā devatā vatthūni pariggaṇhanti, nīcānaṁ tattha raññaṁ rājamahāmattānaṁ cittāni namanti nivesanāni māpetuṁ. | T: In the area where devas of low influence occupied sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of low influence were inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:16.5 | S: The Buddha rose at the crack of dawn and addressed Ānanda, | T: Then, getting up in the last watch of the night, the Blessed One addressed Ven. Ānanda,
ud8.6:17.1 | S: “Ānanda, who is building a citadel at Pāṭali Village?” | T: “Ānanda, who is building a city at Pāṭali Village?”
ud8.6:17.2 | S: “Sir, the Magadhan chief ministers Sunidha and Vassakāra are building a citadel to keep the Vajjis out.” | T: “Sunīdha & Vassakāra, the chief ministers of Magadha, lord, are building a city at Pāṭali Village to preempt the Vajjians.”
ud8.6:17.3 | S: “It’s as if they were building the citadel in consultation with the gods of the thirty-three. | T: “Ānanda, it’s as if they had consulted the Devas of the Thirty-three:
ud8.6:17.4 | P: evamevaṁ kho, ānanda, sunidhavassakārā magadhamahāmattā pāṭaligāme nagaraṁ māpenti vajjīnaṁ paṭibāhāya. | T: That’s how Sunīdha & Vassakāra, the chief ministers of Magadha, are building a city at Pāṭali Village to preempt the Vajjians.
ud8.6:17.5 | S: With clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman, I saw those deities taking possession of building sites. | T: “Just now, Ānanda–with the divine eye–purified and surpassing the human–I saw many devas by the thousands occupying sites in Pāṭali Village.
ud8.6:17.6 | S: Illustrious rulers or royal chief ministers inclined to build houses at sites possessed by illustrious deities. | T: In the area where devas of great influence occupy sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of great influence are inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:17.7 | S: Middling rulers or royal chief ministers inclined to build houses at sites possessed by middling deities. | T: In the area where devas of middling influence occupy sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of middling influence are inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:17.8 | S: Lesser rulers or royal chief ministers inclined to build houses at sites possessed by lesser deities. | T: In the area where devas of low influence occupy sites, there the minds of the king’s royal ministers of low influence are inclined to build their homes.
ud8.6:17.9 | S: As far as the civilized region extends, as far as the trading zone extends, this will be the chief city: the Pāṭaliputta trade center. | T: “Ānanda, as far as the sphere of the Ariyans extends, as far as merchants’ roads extend, this will be the supreme city: Pāṭaliputta, where the seedpods of the Pāṭali plant break open.

